A||||16||Alex4mula||88 Formula lowering results||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||10-08-2001||09:21 AM||||Well finally got rid of the 4x4 look of my Formula. Installed a new rack & pinion, KYB shocks around and cut the springs. Yep. You heard right. My other Formula has Fiero Store springs and I wanted to see what was the difference. If I don't like the ride then I can change them later. I cut 1.5 coils of the front and 1 coil and 5 inches (measured from the start of the top coil) of the back ones (about 1 1/4 coils). Originally the car had a gap of about 3" all around. This lowered the car about 1 1/4" all around. I was surprised that the results were almost exact front and back. I love the look. Haven't driven it much but the ride feels a little bit stiffer than the FS springs which I expected. Not too bad. Took it to alignment shop this morning so by noon I'll see more if it is worth the $250 savings. It developed a vibration on acceleration, which I attribute to rear alignment. Will see that then too. You can see pics of before and after here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ofoto.com/I.jsp?m=88135992203&n=1221239614" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ofoto.com/I.jsp?m=88135992203&n=1221239614</A> <P>Sorry, PIP doesn't work with my firewall.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||10-08-2001||09:29 AM||||Looks good, Alex. Please keep us updated. <BR>I'm thinking about lowering mine, as well. Don't really want the Fiero Store springs (I've heard more than one person say they collapse, over time.), and the only place (I think) I can get Eibachs is ACE. Pricey.<P>Anyone know for sure if the 88 coupe springs are softer than Formula springs? If I decide to cut springs, I can go either way.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||10-08-2001||10:16 AM||||Looks Great!||||reg||14
Z||000003||SLYGUY||10-08-2001||11:20 AM||||Looks great Alex. It will feel much different. Be prepared to have the car definately feel lowered at all corners. I think you will like it. <P>Pontiac put great suspension on the 88s, but wow is it ever high. You feel like you could forge streams or something!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <P>Best of luck with the car. I think it looks great!<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||10-08-2001||11:39 AM||||Alex, Looks great, I just emailed photos to your hotmail address, do I need to resend to your other one? <BR>Coy||||reg||14
Z||000005||FieroMaster88||10-08-2001||12:01 PM||||Wow!  That looks great!  Let me know how the ride is with those. I may have to do that with the coupe.  I really don't want to get the Fiero Store's springs and this looks like a good alternative.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||14
Z||000006||Master_Sushi||10-08-2001||12:10 PM||||I thought the 88 were a little lower stock them pre 88. I can tell when I had my 88 coupe next to my 84SE. My 88 coupe was almost an inch lower. Both were stock setups.||||reg||5
Z||000007||Formula||10-08-2001||12:22 PM||||when Ipark next to pre-88s the rear of mine seems lower and front sems about the same.  it looks lopsided lol..||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wannabe||10-08-2001||12:40 PM||||Tanks for the clear before/after comparison pics Alex. This is wierd though...I also have  a white formula and my name is Alex.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||10-08-2001||12:55 PM||||Thanks all for the compliments  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Well, picked the car from the alignment shop and tested it going through train tracks, bumps and the highway. I'm very happy with the results  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> It feels planted on the floor with minimal sway. The rear is definitely a little stiffer than my other Formula with FS springs but completely acceptable. Specially for the $avings.<BR>Now, the vibration continues  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Is like a drivetrain vibration from 2500 rpm to 3500. I know this has nothing to do with the lowering because is just a normal 1"+ drop, but definitely is something that was highlighted by it. Maybe is a CV joint or tranny related. It only does it if I accelerate at part throttle or hard. If I start slowly it won't do it. Before the lowering it had a similar vibration that came and go and was more noticeable when carrying a passenger (added weight?). I thought it was TV solenoid related but now I don't know. Oh well... Any ideas of what to check? TIA||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||10-08-2001||03:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Anyone know for sure if the 88 coupe springs are softer than Formula springs? <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The front springs are softer, but the rears on almost all '88s are identical.<P>The front coupe springs are usually ARC while the Formula/GT's are usually ARD.  Some GT's (and maybe some Formulas) had even stiffer front springs....I think the code is FJH.  My 16K mile '88 GT has these springs.<P>Almost all rear springs, whether it is a coupe, Formula or GT, are PUC.<P>I have a few sets of ARC & ARD front springs as well as several sets of PUC rears.  So if anyones wants to try cutting theirs down and doesn't like it they can replace them with stock springs.   ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wolfhound||10-08-2001||03:41 PM||||Alex, Try unplugging the lock up solenoid and running up to 3500 for a test. That will at least eliminate it as a problem.<BR>Coy ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||10-08-2001||04:32 PM||||Wolfhound; I did that as soon as I felt the vib. No change. Today I'll try to jack it up and inspect the axels and see if I can find something.||||reg||1
Z||000013||West Coast Fiero||10-08-2001||05:03 PM||||probable cause could be worn carrier bearings in your 5 spd transmission. Unfortunatley the Getrag/Muncie 5spd was designed with smaller than required carrier bearing which causes the bearings to wear overtime. A way to check is to grab the Cv tri-pod right at the transmission out put and try to move the axle side to side, if there is movement the bearings must be replaced<P><P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex4mula||10-08-2001||05:11 PM||||West; My tranny is an automatic. Could that happen in that too?||||reg||1
Z||000015||qwikgta||10-08-2001||08:54 PM||||Alex...WOW great timing, I too just got my 88 on the road.  Over the last two weekends I put on KYB's and also cut the coils on all 4 corners.  I also installed a 88 GT rear swaybar on my coupe.  I ended up with a 1 1/4 front and 1 1/8 in the rear drop but it looked like so much more.  I need to get it in for a allinnment and then try it out.  <P>One thought, I noticed that now the rear suspention on my car is actually horizonal and a little higher than before.  (make sense)  what I mean is that b4 the cut coil the lower control parts were angled down a bit and now after the cut it is going up.  Maybe on your car because of the same angle it is causing a vibration.  I remember when I worked on rear wheel drive cars the angle of the driveshaft was important and you never wanted the driveshaft to go in to the rearend in a straight line, you wanted some angle.  Maybe your shafts are now going into the wheel with no angle on them causeing a vibe.  Just an idea, not sure if you understand because i am not doing a good job of writting this, I know what I mean to say.  <P>Good luck<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000016||Alex4mula||10-08-2001||09:35 PM||||Well, my expertly friend checked the axels and we found that the CV joins are gone. We guess the angle change made more noticeable the situation which was there before. My other car has the same drop and thus the same angle so it is not that. Anyone knows were to get good cheap rebuild axels? Tks.||||reg||1
